title: 'pallet-revive: split the call and instantiate benchmarks by value transfer'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `seal_call`, `seal_instantiate` and `evm_instantiate` now measure the operation without a
    value transfer. Each of the three also gets a new benchmark that measures it with a value
    transfer. `WeightInfo` changes accordingly:
    - `seal_call(t, d, i)` becomes `seal_call(i)`
    - `seal_instantiate(t, d, i)` becomes `seal_instantiate(i)`
    - `evm_instantiate(t, d, i)` becomes `evm_instantiate(i)`
    - `seal_call_transfer(d)`, `seal_instantiate_transfer(d)` and `evm_instantiate_transfer(d)`
      are new

    A runtime with its own `pallet_revive` weights has to re-run the benchmarks.

    The `seal_call` benchmark now measures the per-byte cost of cloning the input with the
    `CLONE_INPUT` flag, while previously it always cloned an empty input.
    Unlike the previous `seal_call` benchmark, `seal_call_transfer` also covers the cost of
    creating the callee's account.
    `call_with_evm_code_per_byte` now benchmarks code up to `MAX_CODE_SIZE`.
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    The weight charged for calls and instantiations changes. With the pallet's default weights:
    - A call without value costs less and does one fewer storage read. Cloning the input with
      the `CLONE_INPUT` flag costs about 150 picoseconds per byte instead of 3.
    - The weight of a call with value now includes creating the callee's account.
    - The weights of a PolkaVM instantiate and of an EVM `CREATE` or `CREATE2` change, with and
      without value.
    - An instantiate, `CREATE` or `CREATE2` that sends only dust now pays the full transfer cost,
      as a call already does.

    Runtimes with their own weights see different numbers.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: patch
